// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the real tanya-yi series > renders the course map (snapshot) 1`] = `
{
  "h1": "Tanya (Yiddish)",
  "keyThemes": [
    "The two souls: the G-dly soul and the animal soul from kelipas nogah",
    "All Jewish souls as one body and one rope across the generations",
    "Uniting with Hashem through Torah study and mitzvos",
    "Tzaddik, rasha and beinoni: the mind ruling the heart",
    "The hidden love from the Avos, rooted in chochmah above understanding",
    "Mesirus nefesh, and sin as a spirit of folly akin to idolatry",
    "Elevating permitted things and teshuvah out of love",
    "Joy against sadness and foreign thoughts in the daily battle",
  ],
  "label": "Grouped and named by AI across all the shiurim. Not reviewed by a person.",
  "overview": "In this Yiddish series the Rav learns through the first part of the Tanya, from the oath to be a tzaddik in chapter one to the dullness of the heart in chapter 29. He presents the Tanya as a 'book of beinonim', a short path meant for small people. He begins with the two souls: the animal soul from kelipas nogah, which can be raised to holiness, and the G-dly soul, 'truly a part of G-d above'. All souls, he says, form one body and one rope through the generations, down to our own heel before Moshiach. He then goes through the ten powers of the soul, the garments of thought, speech and deed, and the union with Hashem that Torah study brings. The middle shiurim set out the levels of tzaddik, rasha and beinoni: the beinoni never sins, because the mind rules the heart, even though his evil is still present. His signature teaching follows in chapters 18 and 19: every Jew carries a hidden love from the Avos, rooted in chochmah above understanding, which is why even the simplest Jew gives his life rather than deny Hashem, and he sins only through a spirit of folly. The closing shiurim give practical counsel: fight the yetzer hara with joy and zerizus, keep sadness over sins for set times, dismiss foreign thoughts with 'ein od milvado', and break the heart's coarseness through cheshbon hanefesh.",
  "rows": [
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "1",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-01",
      "text": "The Rav opens the Tanya as a 'book of beinonim': a short path for small people, who can still reach deep Torah learning through the hidden love inherited from the Avos.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "2",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-01",
      "text": "Starting the sefer itself, the Rav introduces its author and the first question of chapter one: why a soul is sworn to be a tzaddik yet to see itself as a rasha.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "3",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-02",
      "text": "Mazal decides a person's physical traits but not whether he is a tzaddik or a rasha, which leads to the Tanya's definition of a tzaddik as one with no yetzer hara.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "4",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-03",
      "text": "The foundation is laid: every Jew has two souls, and the Rav explains the names sitra achra and klipah and the four klipos that parallel the four elements.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "5",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-04",
      "text": "The animal soul of a Jew comes from kelipas nogah, which can be elevated, and all bad middos stem from its four elements.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "6",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-05",
      "text": "Completing chapter one, the Rav contrasts kelipas nogah with the three impure klipos of the nations, which have no tikkun until Moshiach.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "7",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-06",
      "text": "Turning to the G-dly soul, 'truly a part of G-d above', the Rav explains levels among souls as one body from head to heel, down to our heel before Moshiach.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "8",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-07",
      "text": "On chapter two: all souls together form one body like Adam haRishon's, and their differences come only from the descent, not from their essence.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "9",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-08",
      "text": "The one-body image becomes 'Yaakov chevel nachalaso': all Jewish souls form one rope, each drawing from and affecting all the others.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "10",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-09",
      "text": "Every Jew draws his life through bittul to the talmidei chachamim, and the parents' holiness shapes the body and its mazal but never the soul itself.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "11",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-10",
      "text": "Each part of the soul is made of ten powers from the ten sefiros, and the Rav goes through them one by one as they show up in a person.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "12",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-11",
      "text": "On chapter three: intellect as 'mothers' and middos as 'doubles', and chochmah as the hidden point in every Jew that sin never reaches.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "13",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-12",
      "text": "Chochmah and binah, 'father and mother' of the mind, give birth to love and fear of Hashem through long contemplation, the Ba'al HaTanya's distinctive path.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "14",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-13",
      "text": "From the powers to their garments: a Jew must fulfill all 613 mitzvos in thought, speech and deed, completing himself through Klal Yisrael and the tzaddik where he cannot alone.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "15",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-14",
      "text": "On chapter four: Torah study clothes the soul like the mitzvos, each Jew according to his understanding and the root of his soul.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "16",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-15",
      "text": "Through Torah and mitzvos a Jew unites with Hashem Himself, since Hashem contracted His will and wisdom into the halachos, a union greater than Olam Haba.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "17",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-16",
      "text": "With the Derech Hashem and chapter five: Torah draws down G-dliness only when learned with awe by an upright Jew, and understanding a halacha unites one most deeply.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "18",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-17",
      "text": "Chapter six turns to the other side: ten impure 'crowns' opposite the ten powers of kedushah, and how permitted things done for Heaven raise kelipas nogah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "19",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-18",
      "text": "Holiness is whatever is nullified to Hashem and receives face to face, while the sitra achra is a self-standing 'something' that receives only from behind.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "20",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-19",
      "text": "The body, food and worldly matters from klipas nogah can be turned to holiness, while what comes from the three wholly impure klipos cannot.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "21",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-20",
      "text": "Every Jew has his own portion in the Torah and a hidden inner point of closeness to Hashem, which is why learning shelo lishmah leads to lishmah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "22",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-21",
      "text": "On chapter seven: sins rooted in the three impure kelipos are elevated only through teshuvah out of great love, and even wasted seed can be repaired through sincere teshuvah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "23",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-22",
      "text": "Permitted worldly things from kelipas nogah are raised by using them for Heaven, as Yitzchak found living water in the wells he dug.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "24",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-23",
      "text": "Teshuvah out of love draws a flash of the days of Moshiach into a person's world, which is why it repairs even what is called beyond repair.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "25",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-24",
      "text": "On chapter eight: the lasting imprint of physical pleasure and idle talk, the severity of bitul Torah, and why secular wisdom for its own sake is worse than idle talk.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "26",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-25",
      "text": "Chapter ten begins the levels of avodah: the tzaddik gamur who turned evil to good, the tzaddik she'eino gamur whose evil is only subdued, and the bnei aliyah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "27",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-26",
      "text": "The two souls fight like two kings over the 'small city' of the body, and the Rav asks what this war demands of an ordinary person.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "28",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-27",
      "text": "Chapters 11 and 12 bring the rasha v'tov lo, the rasha v'ra lo, and at last the beinoni: one who never sins, whose evil is silenced during davening.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "29",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-28",
      "text": "In the beinoni the evil inclination is still present but can never clothe itself in thought, speech or deed, because the mind rules the heart.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "30",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-29",
      "text": "The beinoni is judged by two 'judges', the good and evil inclinations, with Hashem deciding, which answers why even a tzaddik should see himself as a rasha.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "31",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-30",
      "text": "Truth has many levels, so the beinoni's prayer is true by his measure, and he should fight bad thoughts through the hidden love rather than be saddened by them.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "32",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-31",
      "text": "The beinoni who battles constantly versus the one 'who does not serve Him' because learning comes easily: real avodah means going beyond nature and habit.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "33",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-32",
      "text": "Returning to the opening oath, the Rav shows how a beinoni can still 'be a tzaddik': through moments of joy in Hashem, habit that becomes nature, and ibbur.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "34",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-33",
      "text": "On chapter 16: a beinoni who cannot feel love of Hashem must know it in his mind, and every Jew serves by the root of his soul, like a letter in its place in the Sefer Torah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "35",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-34",
      "text": "Love and fear understood in the mind become the wings that lift the beinoni's Torah and mitzvos, keeping avodah 'very near' even without feeling.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "36",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-35",
      "text": "On chapter 17: the promise 'close, in your mouth and your heart' is kept through mind-born love and fear that lead to action, open to all but a rasha before teshuvah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "37",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-37",
      "text": "Chapter 18 introduces the series' central teaching: every Jew, even without contemplation, carries a hidden love inherited from the Avos, the chariot of the Shechinah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "38",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-38",
      "text": "Back on chapter 17: the mind rules the heart by nature, so service from understanding is within reach, unless sin has removed that control and broken-hearted teshuvah must come first.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "39",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-39",
      "text": "Every Jewish soul is rooted in chochmah, where the light of Ein Sof rests above understanding, and so even the simplest Jew gives his life rather than deny Hashem.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "40",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-02",
      "text": "The hidden love from the Avos is an instinct as natural as the inherited knowledge of animals, because the Avos were a chariot for the Shechinah.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "41",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-03",
      "text": "Love and fear can live together only with Hashem; the Avos' union with Him passed to their children, and the Rav sketches the four worlds and ten sefiros.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "42",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-04",
      "text": "G-d's light descends through the four worlds and levels of souls, yet beneath every difference each Jew holds the same inner point from the Avos, revealed in the round dance and on Yom Kippur.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "43",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-05",
      "text": "Great souls are born to simple Jews and the darkest times bring the light of Moshiach, as Chanukah does, because chochmah, the source of binah, is beyond understanding.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "44",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-06",
      "text": "Emunah from chochmah means most Jews will give their lives for Hashem, unless, as the Tzemach Tzedek notes, a connection to the goy has cut them off.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "45",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-07",
      "text": "Emunah above understanding also explains why bris milah is done at eight days and how the soul is 'the candle of Hashem'.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "46",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-40",
      "text": "Chapter 19 gives the image: the soul's readiness for mesirus nefesh is a nature above reason, like a flame straining upward to its root.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "47",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-41",
      "text": "Even the lowest Jew would die rather than deny Hashem because of the hidden love; he sins only because a spirit of folly enters him.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "48",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-08",
      "text": "The longing to be nullified like a candle in a torch comes from chochmah, whose essence is bittul, while the sitra achra is self-importance even in giving.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "49",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-09",
      "text": "A sinner drags his G-dly soul into exile, yet its root in chochmah stays untouched and wakes in a test of emunah, so even he gives his life for kiddush Hashem.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "50",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-42",
      "text": "Sins tied to idolatry, like chametz on Pesach and niddah, weaken mesirus nefesh, and chapter 20 opens: every sin is rooted in idolatry, and Hashem remains as before creation.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "51",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-43",
      "text": "On chapter 21: Hashem's speech is one with His essence, and the tzimtzumim and concealments exist only from the side of the creations.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "52",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-44",
      "text": "On chapter 23: the 248 mitzvos are the King's limbs, so one who does a mitzvah becomes a chariot for His will, and one who learns Torah is truly united with Him.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "53",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-45",
      "text": "Torah unites even more than mitzvos, and by contrast a sin cuts a Jew off from Hashem's will, making him worse than the sitra achra, which never rebels.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "54",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-46",
      "text": "On chapter 24: sin is lower than the kelipos, this world is mostly bitter except for true servants, and a spirit of folly hides the love that would refuse idolatry.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "55",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-10",
      "text": "Every sin, even small, is truly avodah zarah and places the sinner below a gnat, and the Rav explains why pikuach nefesh still overrides most sins.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "56",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-11",
      "text": "The practical turn: the same mesirus nefesh a Jew would show against idolatry can carry him through every daily battle with the yetzer hara.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "57",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-1-36",
      "text": "On chapter 26: the yetzer hara is defeated only through zerizus born of simcha, and sadness over sins belongs to set times, 'by appointment only'.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "58",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-12",
      "text": "Sadness lets the yetzer hara win; a set time of broken-heartedness leads to greater joy, and sadness in the middle of business or avodah is the yetzer's trick.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "59",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-13",
      "text": "On chapter 27: a bad thought pushed away is a chance to fulfill 'velo sasuru', and the beinoni's struggle gives Hashem a nachas ruach of its own.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "60",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-14",
      "text": "On chapter 28: foreign thoughts in davening are part of the lifelong war of the two souls, won by dismissing them with joy and 'ein od milvado'.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "61",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-15",
      "text": "On chapter 29: why the heart becomes as dull as stone, why this is so common today, and how to break the body's coarseness so the neshamah's light can take hold.",
    },
    {
      "line": "arc",
      "order": "62",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-16",
      "text": "Continuing chapter 29: why one suddenly cannot daven, why a higher level or holier time demands deeper teshuvah, and breaking the sitra achra through cheshbon hanefesh.",
    },
    {
      "line": "none",
      "order": "63",
      "shiur": "yi-tanya-2-17",
      "text": null,
    },
  ],
  "title": "Tanya: The Two Souls, the Beinoni and the Hidden Love",
}
`;
